      "question_en": "As from last week, a 40 year-old male has had very severe coughing bouts as if choking. When the cough stopped, it was immediately followed by wheezing associated with a distending sensation radiating upward from the lung. The cough causes pain in the chest and hypochondrium. The cough is worse when lying down. The cough expectorates thick sputum, sometimes with small blood clots. He reports being thirsty with a bitter mouth taste. His appetite is poor, has lassitude, constipation for two days, and yellow urine. He describes his skin feeling hot over his whole body. The tongue is red with a yellow coating. The pulse is surging-forceful. Blood Pressure is 130/90, Pulse 80, Respirations 20, and Temperature is 100 F. CBC shows White Blood Count is 15,000; RBC, Hematocrit and Hemoglobin are within normal limits. Chest x-ray is negative for infiltrates. Throat culture is positive for pertussis bordetella. The patient is treated with REN-22 (tian tu), ST-25 (tian shu), LU-6 (kong zui) on one side, and LU-5 (chi ze) on one side. After several treatments the patient had mild improvement that only lasted a short time and then the symptoms returned full force. How would you modify the prescription?",
