      "exp_en": "Intercostal neuralgia is seen as a disorder of the Liver and Gall Bladder channels as a result of bottled emotions such as anger causing the Liver qi to rebel in a lateral direction. If the intercostal neuralgia becomes chronic the qi stasis can lead to blood stasis. The base prescription for intercostal neuralgia is TB-6, GB-34, and the jia ji points. GB-34 (he-sea, earth, and sinew meeting point) spreads Liver qi and benefits the costal area. TB-6 (jing-river and fire point) benefits the chest and lateral costal areas and is indicated for channel disorders. Combined with these two distal points, the jia ji as local points are selected to regulate the affected spinal nerves. LR-3 and PC-6 were chosen under the assumption that underlying disharmony (root) was mainly due to Liver qi stasis. However, the symptoms (fixed, sharp stabbing pain worse at night) pointed to blood stasis as the root of the problem. As such, BL-17 (invigorates and moves the blood), BL-18 (regulates Liver blood), and the local point LR-14 (regulates Liver qi and Blood) were added to the base prescription. LR-2 would be selected if the Liver qi stasis transformed into Fire. SP-6 and SP-9 would be selected if the pattern had a component of dampness which needed to be transformed. Acupuncture A Comprehensive Text, Bensky, 1981, page 617",
