      "exp_en": "The first sign to appear in Dupuytren's contracture is a thickened plaque over the flexor tendon on the palm side of the ring finger. It can also involve the little finger. The skin in the affected area of the palm gets thicker with the skin puckering. As the plaque thickens it brings the ring or little finger closer and closer to the palm, in a bent contracted position. The cause of a Dupuytren's contracture is unknown. What is known is that it frequently occurs in patients with diabetes mellitus, epilepsy, alcoholism, and can also be inherited. Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 13th Edition, page 8334; Bate's Guide to Physical Examination, 10th Edition, page 650",
