      "exp_en": "The the formula tiao wei cheng qi tang (regulate the stomach and order the qi decoction) treats excess yang ming fu (hollow organ) syndrome with the accumulation of excess heat in the Stomach and Large Intestine. It treats yang ming with dryness and heat in the Stomach and Intestines. There is dryness and hardness but without distention and fullness. It is an associated formula of da cheng qi tang (major order the qi decoction). The formula tiao wei cheng qi tang mildly purges clumped heat with mild constipation in the yang ming stage. The formula regulates and harmonizes Stomach qi by removing obstructions. INGREDIENTS: da huang (radix et rhizoma rhei), mang xiao (mirabilitum), and gan cao (radix glycyrrhizae uralensis). The herb gan cao in this formula replaces the herbs zhi shi and hou po in da cheng qi tang. The fully cooked herb da huang slowly clears heat and purges constipated stool. The herb mang xiao clears heat, moistens dryness, and softens the stool. The herb gan cao tonifies qi and harmonizes the formula. The three herbs work together to drain damp-heat and clear out the jaundice. CONTRAINDICATION: The physician He Lian-Chen has a list of eight contraindications as a guide: 1) contraindicated when a pathogen remains at the wei qi stage or in the tai yang level, 2) contraindicated when there is hardness in the epigastric area indicating that the pathogen is still above the diaphragm, 3) contraindicated when there is a flushed face due to Heat Floating in the Exterior, 4) contraindicated for those with Spleen and Stomach Deficiency who eat only small amounts, 5) contraindicated for those who stop eating, 6) contraindicated for those who vomit frequently due to the pathogen remaining in the shao yang, 7) contraindicated for those who have Interior Cold with a slow pulse, 8) contraindicated for those with spontaneous sweating and normal urination. Formulas and Strategies, Bensky, 2nd Edition, page 67; Chinese Herbal Formulas, Chen and Chen, page 161",
