      "exp_en": "This is the most complete and accurate answer. This Zhu Dan-Xi formula treats liver qi stagnation and the various types of constraint that can arise due to it. E zhu is a warm herb from the invigorate blood category. It also promotes the movement of qi but is primarily a strong blood mover capable of breaking up and dissolving accumulations. Mu xiang and wu yao are strong qi regulators capable of regulating the qi in the entire body. Wu yao breaks up qi stagnation in the chest, abdomen, and lower abdomen while also warming the kidneys. This is particularly good herb for pain associated with qi stagnation. Mu xiang moves the qi in the middle burner and intestines while also strengthening the spleen. Yu jin is a blood invigorator but has a strong function to regulate the liver qi and is cold to counter the warmth from the other herbs and any heat that depresses from constraint. Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 630, 609, 529, 534; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 507-510",
      "exp_hant": "此為最完整且準確的答案。此為朱丹溪所創之方劑，主治肝氣鬱結及其所引起的各種鬱證。莪朮為活血類的溫性藥物，亦能行氣，但主要作用為強力活血，能破散並消解積聚。木香與烏藥皆為強效理氣藥，能調理全身之氣。烏藥能破散胸、腹及小腹之氣滯，並能溫腎，對氣滯所致的疼痛尤為適用。木香能行中焦及腸道之氣，並兼能健脾。鬱金為活血藥，但具有強力疏肝理氣的作用，其性寒，可制約其他藥物的溫性及鬱結所生之熱。Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 630, 609, 529, 534; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 507-510"
