      "exp_en": "This formula was originally written for children exhibiting signs of heart heat. It is currently used to drain heat from the heart channel out through the small intestine. When there is deficient yin, care must be taken using a formula like dao chi san which can be damaging with the inclusion of mu tong and dan zhu ye as ingredients. These promote urination and can lead to yin deficiency. In this circumstance, the yin and fluids must be protected through nourishing while maintaining a heat clearing focus that guides the main formula. Shi hu and zhi mu are added for their ability to nourish the yin and clear heat. It might seem that an herb like bai he would be better in this case considering its ability to clear the heart but it only nourishes lung yin as opposed to shi hu which nourishes kidney yin. Zhi mu drains fire while nourishing the fluids and yin as well. As a combination, they have very strong potential to clear heat while protecting and nourishing the yin and fluids of the body. Mai men dong is able to clear heart heat fairly well and described as a \"refrigerant of the heart\", but it nourishes the yin of the lung and stomach and not the kidney yin. Sheng di huang is not a bad choice for this situation considering its ability to cool the blood while nourishing the yin and fluids. It could be argued that shu di huang would be an even better choice is this situation. Jue ming zi clears the liver and subdues liver yang rising but has the ability to nourish yin and moisten the intestines. This function is mostly related to the functioning of the eyes and is not a good choice for nourishing the liver and kidney yin in general. Sha shen nourishes the lung and stomach yin and clears lung heat. E jiao strongly nourishes the blood and nourishes the yin as well. This is a good choice from the nourishing yin perspective but it does not clear heat to protect the yin. Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 111, 828, 92, 822, 120, 816, 756; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 195-196",
      "exp_hant": "本方原為治療小兒心熱之證而設，現今則用於清心經之熱、使其由小腸而出。當有陰虛之情況時，須謹慎使用如導赤散這類方劑，因方中木通、淡竹葉具利尿之性，可能耗傷陰液，導致陰虛加重。此時須在維持清熱主軸之同時，兼顧滋養陰液。加石斛、知母即為取其滋陰清熱之功。或許有人認為百合較適合此證，因其能清心，但百合僅能滋養肺陰，而石斛則能滋養腎陰。知母瀉火之餘亦能滋養津液與陰分。兩藥合用，清熱之力甚強，同時能護陰生津。麥門冬雖能相當程度地清心熱，被稱為「心之清涼劑」，但其滋養的是肺胃之陰而非腎陰。生地黃用於此證亦非壞選擇，因其能涼血兼滋陰生津，甚或可論熟地黃於此情況更佳。決明子清肝、平肝陽上亢，兼能滋陰潤腸，惟其功用多與眼目相關，並非滋養肝腎之陰的普遍良藥。沙參滋養肺胃之陰並清肺熱。阿膠能強力滋血養陰，就滋陰角度而言是不錯的選擇，惟其不具清熱護陰之功。Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 111, 828, 92, 822, 120, 816, 756; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 195-196"
    },
