      "exp_en": "This case currently has a pattern of external cool dryness which obstructs the functions of the lungs. This invasion obstructs the circulation of the wei qi and fluids leading to the accumulation of thin fluid or mucus, in addition, chills and headache without the presence of sweating are a good indication of cool dryness attacking the exterior. For this pattern, xing su san is the correct prescription. Sheng mai san treats qi and yin deficiency of the lung. Coughing is a very common symptom for this pattern and headache is a possible complication however the causation is an internal deficiency while this patient is suffering from an external attack. It is possible that sheng mai san may be a formula that the patient will need during their recovery, however, it is not currently indicated. Zhi sou san treats a lingering cough as a result of a sequelae of an external attack. After an external attack resolves, coughing can linger as the patient recovers. This patient has not yet resolved the external attack as evidenced by the floating pulse. Qing qi hua tan tang treats internal phlegm heat after internal fire has cooked the fluids down causing stagnation as the deficiency of the spleen has diminished its ability to transport the fluids. For this patient, there is no evidence of fire or phlegm. The signs and symptoms of this patient indicated a cold pathogen. Furthermore, the floating pulse indicates that this is an exterior syndrome and qing qi hua tan tang is an internal formula. Bensky, Dan, et.al., Formulas and Strategies. Pgs.663, 328, 815, 790",
