      "exp_en": "Both yu ping feng san and gui zhi tang treat exterior deficiency with spontaneous sweating and aversion to wind. The symptoms yu ping feng san are due to qi deficiency with an inability to consolidate the exterior. The formula treats leakages of body fluids, frequent catching of colds, generalized weakness due to exterior deficiency. In such cases the normal Qi is not strong enough to defend the body against external wind. It is a formula designed for patients who have not yet contracted an external condition. On the other hand, gui zhi tang treats acute fevers and colds due to wind-cold attack. It is used for cases in which sweating will not expel the pathogen when there is a disharmony between the wei qi and ying qi. It is formulated to treat patients who are suffering from an external wind-cold invasion. Despite their differences, these two. Formulas can be used together or interchangeably for cases in which a severely Qi deficient patient may not be able to withstand a strongly dispersing formula. In addition to spontaneous sweating, the indications for yu ping feng san include aversion to drafts, a shiny-pale complexion, pale tongue with white coat, and a floating, deficient, and soft pulse. It is contraindicated for excess patterns. The indications for gui zhi tang include headache, nasal congestion, dry heaves, stiff neck, and a floating, deficient pulse. Neither formula is useful for cases of wind-heat.",
