      "exp_en": "Long dan cao and mu dan pi are added to the original prescription to treat liver fire. The insomnia along with the wiry pulse suggests that fire or heat from the liver is rising to disturb the heart. Soreness in the low back and knees without the presence of other specific yin deficiency signs suggests that these symptoms are due to fire from the liver disturbing the body's yin. Chai Hu moves qi but is not strong enough to counter liver fire. It is also drying which can further damage the yin. Huang qi tonifies the blood and qi and although the signs and symptoms suggest that there is some dryness, the tongue and pulse suggests an excess condition. Bai ji li and ju hua can cool the liver and move qi but neither is sufficiently draining of liver fire for this case. Ban xia and ju hong regulate the qi and resolve phlegm-dampness. Both of these herbs are drying in nature which is not a good combination in the presence of fire. Formulas and Strategies, 2nd Edition, pgs. 647-649.",
