      "exp_en": "This combination is the greater part of dao chi san (add gan cao shao) which treats excess heart fire for symptoms such as irritability, mouth ulcers, heat sensation in the chest, red face, and burning sore throat (due to the heart channel going through the throat). Mu tong serves as the chief herb with its bitter and cold qualities that clears heat from the heart and promotes urination below which also clears heat through the small intestine. Dan zhu ye clears heart heat thereby relieving irritability. Sheng di huang enters the heart to cool the blood and nourishes the yin and fluids. This combination would be able to treat painful urination due to heart fire but mu tong is the only one of these herbs which would be beneficial for edema. Dao chi san is cautioned for cases when there is spleen qi deficiency, of which loose stools is a characteristic symptom. For lin syndrome, with scanty urine, more emphasis should be on removing the damp heat obstruction and tonification of the yin and fluids. Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 195-196",
      "exp_hant": "此組合為導赤散之主要組成（再加甘草梢），治心火亢盛所致之心煩、口舌生瘡、胸中熱感、面赤及咽喉灼痛（因心經循行咽喉）。木通為君藥，性苦寒，能清心火並利小便，兼清小腸之熱。淡竹葉能清心火而除心煩。生地黃入心經，能涼血並滋養陰液。此組合能治心火所致之小便疼痛，然三藥中唯有木通對水腫有益。導赤散於脾氣虛（以大便溏瀉為特徵）之患者應謹慎使用。若淋證見小便量少，則更應著重袪除濕熱阻滯並滋補陰液。《方劑學》第二版，Bensky著，2009年，第195-196頁"
    },
    {
      "number": 444,
