    {
      "number": 454,
      "question_en": "A 37 year old man complains of neck ache and stiffness. He also has fever, chills and no sweating. He reports also he has been having diarrhea with no known cause. He wears a hoodie to block the wind and rubs his eyes which he claims are dry. His pulse is superficial. Which diagnosis is correct and which treatment would be the best choice?",
      "question_hant": "一名37歲男性主訴頸部痠痛僵硬，並有發熱、惡寒、無汗，且訴說近來原因不明地腹瀉。他戴著連帽外套以遮擋風邪，並不斷揉眼，自述雙眼乾澀。脈浮。何種診斷正確，且何種治療為最佳選擇？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "Cold Damage disease Ma Huang Fu Zi Tang (Ephedra, Prepared Aconite and Asarum Decoction) Treatment",
          "hant": "傷寒病，麻黃附子細辛湯治療",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "Tai yang disease Ge Gen Tang (Kudzu Decoction) treatment",
          "hant": "太陽病，葛根湯治療",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "Tai yang disease Ma Huang Tang (Ephedra Decoction) treatment",
          "hant": "太陽病，麻黃湯治療",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Wind Strike Gui Zhi Fu Ling Wan (Cinnamon and Hoelen Combination) treatment",
          "hant": "中風，桂枝茯苓丸治療",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "In the Shang Han Lun, this is labelled clearly as a Tai Yang disease but not distinguished between a Wind Strike and Cold Damage. Interestingly, Ge Gen Tang is a formula based on Gui Zhi Tang which includes Ma Huang and Ge Gen. The case includes an aversion to wind (a wind strike sign), but also reports no sweating (a cold damage sign). In these cases, the treatment usually dictates how the disease is labelled. Here, wind evil attacks and binds the exterior causing a disruption in the flow of qi and fluids through the cou li and stagnating the ying and wei. With qi and fluids insufficient to nourish the channel, the neck and nape are stiff and pain ensues. Due to the lack of sweating, Ma Huang is given but the dryness suggests a more moderate approach to the sweating so as not to further damage the fluids. Ma Huang Tang is too strong a diaphoretic formula to use in this case. Fu Zi is too hot and damaging to the impaired qi to use and Gui Zhi Fu Ling Wan is for blood stasis in the womb during pregnancy. Shang Han Lun: On Cold Damage, Mitchell, Craig, Ye, Feng, Wiseman, Nigel, 1999, pgs 109-112.",
      "exp_hant": "在《傷寒論》中，此證明確歸類為太陽病，但並未區分中風或傷寒。有趣的是，葛根湯乃以桂枝湯為基礎，再加麻黃與葛根而成。本案例中既有惡風（中風之徵象），又見無汗（傷寒之徵象）。此類情況通常以治法來決定病名之歸類。此處風邪侵襲並鬱閉肌表，致使氣液運行於腠理受阻，營衛壅滯。氣液不足以濡養經脈，故項背強痛。由於無汗，故用麻黃，然其乾燥之象提示發汗宜較為和緩，以免進一步耗傷津液。麻黃湯發汗之力過強，不適用於此案例。附子性過於燥熱，恐傷及已受損之氣，而桂枝茯苓丸則用於妊娠期子宮血瘀之證。《傷寒論：論傷寒》，Mitchell, Craig, Ye, Feng, Wiseman, Nigel著，1999年，第109-112頁。"
    },
    {
      "number": 455,
      "question_en": "A patient comes to the clinic with the following symptoms: slight fever and chills, headache, intolerance to wind, perspiration on slight exertion, general lowered resistance. The pulse is floating and vacuous. You correctly prescribe Gui Zhi Tang (Cinnamon Twig Decoction) for this patient. After a week of taking the formula as directed, the patient remarks that he was unable to achieve any moderate sweats and his condition has changed since going on a fishing trip in the mountains with his son. He now presents with difficulty when breathing deeply, no appetite, coughing, and slight dizziness. Additionally he reports feeling chilled but had a feverish sensation while driving to your office about a half an hour ago. The pulse is wiry and floating. What would you prescribe for the patient's current condition?",
      "question_hant": "一名患者前來就診，症見輕微發熱惡寒、頭痛、畏風、稍動即汗出、整體抵抗力低下。脈浮而虛。醫者正確地予以桂枝湯治療。服藥一週後，患者表示未能達到微微汗出之效果，且在與兒子到山區釣魚後病情發生變化。現症見呼吸深時感困難、食慾不振、咳嗽、輕微頭暈。此外，他表示身覺寒冷，但約半小時前開車來診所途中曾有發熱感。脈弦而浮。針對患者目前之病況，應如何處方？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
