      "exp_en": "The Chief herb is chai hu (bitter, acrid, cool). The Deputy herb is zhi shi (bitter, acrid, slightly cold). The Assistant herb is bai shao (bitter, sour, mild cold). The Envoy herb is zhi gan cao (sweet, warm). The formula vents pathogenic qi, releases constraint, spreads Liver qi, and regulates the Spleen. The chief herb chai hu regulate the qi mechanism by venting heat and releasing constraint. chai hu acts on the shao yang, ascends the clear yang, and descends turbid yin. The deputy herb zhi shi drains stasis and reduces middle jiao accumulations to benefit the Spleen functions of transformation-transportation. The bitter nature of zhi shi directs qi down and complements chai hu which focuses on venting shao yang. Combined these two herbs regulate the qi to ensure a smooth dispersion the heat which arises from the constraint. The assistant herb is bai shao nourishes the Liver and preservers yin. The herb bai shao has holding nature as opposed to chai hu which has a dispersing nature. Combining chai hu with bai shao spreads Liver qi without injuring Liver yin. The combination of the assistant herb bai shao with the envoy herb zhi gan cao moderate acute or colicky pain. The herb zhi gan cao also harmonizes the actions of the other herbs and can tonify the Spleen to help curb the Liver. The formula vents pathogenic heat without injuring the normal qi so that warmth can extend to all four limbs. Formulas and Strategies, Bensky, 2nd Edition, page 116",
      "exp_hant": "君藥為柴胡（苦、辛、涼）。臣藥為枳實（苦、辛、微寒）。佐藥為白芍（苦、酸、微寒）。使藥為炙甘草（甘、溫）。此方透邪解鬱、疏肝理脾。君藥柴胡透熱解鬱、調暢氣機，作用於少陽，升發清陽，降泄濁陰。臣藥枳實泄滯消積，以助脾之運化功能，其苦性主降，與偏於透散少陽之柴胡相輔相成，二藥合用調理氣機，使鬱熱得以順利透散。佐藥白芍養肝斂陰，其性主斂，與柴胡之散性相反，柴胡與白芍相配可疏肝而不傷肝陰。佐藥白芍與使藥炙甘草相配可緩急止痛。炙甘草亦調和諸藥，並能補脾以抑肝。此方透散邪熱而不傷正氣，使陽氣得以布達四肢。《方劑學》，Bensky，第二版，第116頁"
    },
