      "exp_en": "The formula zhu ye shi gao tang (lophatherus and gypsum decoction) treats qi-level heat lingering in the Lungs and Stomach where the heat injures the qi and fluids. This formula treats the recovery stage of the aftermath of a febrile disorder. The more pronounced heat has lessened but low-grade fever with sweating remains. This formula clears heat, generates fluids, augments qi, and harmonizes the Stomach. This formula has the ability to clear lurking heat and tonify qi deficiency. The chief herb is shi gao whose cold nature clears heat from the Lungs and Stomach. Its acrid nature vents pathogenic heat to the exterior. Its sweet nature generates fluid to stop thirst. The formula has three deputies. The herb dan zhu ye is sweet-bland-cold and enters the Heart, Lung, and Stomach channels. The herb dan zhu ye clears lingering heat through urination and vents the upper jiao. The other two deputies are ren shen and mai men dong which work together to moisten the Lungs, nourish the yin, benefit the Stomach, generate fluids, and clear Heart heat to eliminate irritability. The assistant herb is zhi ban xia which directs rebellious qi down to stop nausea and vomiting. The herb zhi ban xia is warm-acrid and can be seen as a wrong herb to include in the formula. However, when zhi ban xia is combined with herbs that clear heat and generate fluids, it strengthens Spleen qi and reduces the cloying and stagnating effects of the herb mai men dong. The envoy herbs are zhi gan cao and jing mi assist ren shen in tonifying the qi and harmonizing the middle jiao to protect the Stomach from being injured by shi gao. CONTRAINDICATION: Febrile disorder with abundant normal and pathogenic qi, when the Fever remains high or when there is no damage to qi and yin. Formulas and Strategies, Bensky, 2nd Edition, page 155",
