      ],
      "exp_en": "The formula da cheng qi tang (major order the qi decoction) vigorously purges heat accumulation at the yang ming stage. When constipation is due to excess inner heat the treatment principle is to purge the constipation and to cool the heat. The chief herb in the formula is da huang which breaks up abdominal masses, accumulations, stuck fluids, and stuck food through flushing the Stomach and Intestines. INGREDIENTS: da huang (radix et rhizoma rhei), mang xiao (mirabilitum), hou po (cortex magnoliae officinalis), zhi shi (fructus immaturus citri aurantii). The accumulation of heat can become constrained and can lead to an inversion of heat where the heat constraint becomes so pronounced that the four limbs fail to be supplied with yang qi and become ice-cold. The deputy herb mang xiao assists da huang to soften the stool. The herbs da huang and mang xiao moisten dryness as they drain down. The assistant herb zhi shi clears clumps and reduces focal distention. The other assistant herb hou po moves the qi and alleviates the sensation of abdominal fullness. The root of the pattern is heat which clumps and then stagnates the qi. Both assistants help expel the stool by moving the qi. As whole this formula treats the four types abdominal disorders which are focal distention, fullness, dryness, and excess. The herb zhi shi treats focal distention which is the sensation of distension, obstruction, and heaviness that is focused on the epigastrium. The herb hou po treats fullness which is the visible distention that resists pressure. The herb mang xiao treats dryness which is the dry stool accumulating in the Intestines and causes the lower abdomen to become tense. The herb da huang treats excess which refers to the accumulation of heat that produces constipation and abdominal pain which worsens with pressure. CONTRAINDICATION: The physician He Lian-Chen has a list of eight contraindications as a guide: 1) contraindicated when a pathogen remains at the wei qi stage or in the tai yang level, 2) contraindicated when there is hardness in the epigastric area indicating that the pathogen is still above the diaphragm, 3) contraindicated when there is a flushed face due to Heat Floating in the Exterior, 4) contraindicated for those with Spleen and Stomach Deficiency who eat only small amounts, 5) contraindicated for those who stop eating, 6) contraindicated for those who vomit frequently due to the pathogen remaining in the shao yang, 7) contraindicated for those who have Interior Cold with a slow pulse, 8) contraindicated for those with spontaneous sweating and normal urination. Formulas and Strategies, Bensky, 2nd Edition, page 63",
      "exp_hant": "大承氣湯強力峻下陽明積熱。若便秘乃因裡熱熾盛所致，治則為瀉下通便、清熱。方中君藥大黃，能瀉下腸胃之積聚、瘀滯之津液與宿食，並蕩滌腸胃。組成：大黃、芒硝、厚朴、枳實。熱邪積聚日久可致熱鬱，鬱熱過甚則可致四肢陽氣不得布達而厥冷，此即所謂熱厥。臣藥芒硝助大黃軟堅通便。大黃、芒硝二藥潤燥而瀉下。佐藥枳實消痞除滿；另一佐藥厚朴行氣，緩解腹部脹滿之感。此證之根本在於熱結而致氣滯，二佐藥皆藉行氣以助通便。整體而言，此方治痞、滿、燥、實四大腹證：枳實治「痞」，即胃脘部脹悶阻塞、沉重之感；厚朴治「滿」，即按之抵抗之明顯脹滿；芒硝治「燥」，即腸中糞便乾燥、少腹拘急；大黃治「實」，即熱結所致之便秘與按之加重之腹痛。禁忌：清代醫家何廉臣曾列出八項禁忌以為準則：一、邪尚在衛分或太陽階段者忌用；二、胃脘部尚硬滿，示邪仍在膈上者忌用；三、面赤為熱浮於表者忌用；四、脾胃虛弱、食量甚少者忌用；五、不能進食者忌用；六、頻嘔而邪尚在少陽者忌用；七、裡寒脈遲者忌用；八、自汗而小便正常者忌用。《方劑學》，Bensky，第二版，第63頁"
