          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "Both bi xie and tu fu ling dispel damp, resolve turbid painful urinary dribbling, and resolve sores due to wind-damp. However, bi xie excels at separating the clear from the turbid, and is therefore commonly used for cloudy urinary difficulty or vaginal discharge. On the other hand, tu fu ling is better at resolving toxicity, and may be used for treating noxious sores and eliminating mercury from the body. Bi xie is bitter and neutral, and enters the Urinary Bladder, Liver, and Stomach channels. In addition to clearing dampness from the lower burner, it may be used to treat painful obstruction, numbness, or stiffness due to wind-dampness, although this effect is mild. Tu fu ling is sweet, bland, and neutral, and enters the Liver and Stomach channels. It may be used to clear damp-heat from the skin, and may be used to eliminate dampness from the joints. It also treats damp-heat jaundice and syphilitic sores. Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, 3rd Edition, Bensky, Clavey, and Stoger, pages 199, 200, 301, and 302",
      "exp_hant": "萆薢與土茯苓皆能祛濕、化濁止淋，並治風濕瘡瘍。然而，萆薢尤擅分清別濁，故常用於膏淋或帶下病；相對而言，土茯苓解毒功效較優，可用於治療惡瘡及祛除體內汞毒。萆薢味苦性平，入膀胱、肝、胃經，除清下焦濕邪外，亦可治風濕所致之痺痛、麻木或僵硬，惟此效較弱。土茯苓味甘、淡，性平，入肝、胃經，可清皮膚濕熱，亦可袪除關節濕邪，並治濕熱黃疸與梅毒瘡瘍。《中藥學》，第三版，Bensky、Clavey與Stoger著，第199、200、301、302頁"
