        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "There is no fever present in the symptomology of jia jian wei rui tang",
          "hant": "加減葳蕤湯之證候中並無發熱表現",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "In ren shen bai du san, the body's normal qi is deficient and unable to expel the pathogen. This deficiency of qi allows for dampness to penetrate quickly (accounting for the changing of the tongue coat to greasy in this case). The soggy pulse is also an indicator of the qi deficiency which is a key aspect of this case. The chief herbs of the formula (du huo-radix angelicae pubescentis, qiang huo-rhizoma seu radix notopterygii) both expel wind-cold from the exterior, dispel dampness, and alleviate pain making them good in the treatment of bi syndromes. Chuan xiong (rhizome chuanxiong) helps to release the exterior and regulates the blood helping for the pain symptoms that frequent this pattern. The other deputies, chai hu (radix bulpleurum) and bo he (herba menthae) help to release the wind-cold and free the muscle layer. The qi dynamic is regulated through the usage of opposite movement influences in jie geng (radix platycodi) and zhi ke (fructus aurantii). Jie geng influences movement upwards while zhi ke influences downwards. Qian hu (radix peucedani) and fu ling (poria) are a good combination to expel phlegm while tonifying the Spleen. Sheng jiang (rhizome zingiberis) can treat both the root and branch of the disorder with its ability to regulate the middle while expelling exterior pathogens. It should be noted that three-fourths of the ingredients of si jun zi tang (four gentlemen decoction) appear in this formula (ren shen-radix ginseng, fu ling-poria, and gan cao-radix glycyrrhizae). Jia jian wei rui tang treats a wind-heat contraction with an underlying yin deficiency. Typically, it is not advised to prescribe yin tonic herbs in the presence of an exterior pathogen in fear of trapping the pathogen and forcing into the interior, however, in this case, the yin-fluids are tonified in order to produce a sweat and vent the pathogen from the exterior. Venting the pathogen remains the focus of the formula while simultaneously tonifying the yin. The chief herb yu zhu (rhizoma polygonate odorati) nourishes the yin of the lungs and the stomach while clearing heat. The lungs and the stomach are the only organs in which their yin are fluids. In this, the herb essentially nourishes the fluids of the lungs and stomach. This herb is particularly good at tonifying without the cloying effects as most yin tonics have. The deputies release the exterior of wind-heat (cong bai-bulbus allii fistulosi, jie geng-radix platycodi, bo he-herba menthae, dan dou chi-semen sojae preparatium). Bai wei (radix cynanchi atrati) cools the blood without injuring the yin and fluids and the remaining herbs harmonize the formula and moisten dryness. In this case, both of these formulas treat a base deficiency pattern (ren shen bai du san-deficiency of qi, jia jian wei rui tang-deficiency of yin and fluids) and also show a pattern of no sweating. Because each of these also involves exterior wind invasions, both fever and chills are present to signify the battle between the righteous qi and the pathogenic factor. Formulas and Strategies, Bensky, 2nd Edition, page 58",
      "exp_hant": "人參敗毒散證中，人體正氣不足，無力驅邪外出，此氣虛使濕邪易於迅速侵入（此即本案中舌苔轉為膩苔之原因）。濡脈亦顯示氣虛，此為本證之關鍵所在。方中君藥（獨活、羌活）皆能祛散表之風寒、除濕止痛，故善治痺證。川芎有助解表並調血，可緩解此證常見之疼痛。其餘臣藥柴胡與薄荷，助解風寒、疏通肌表。方中桔梗與枳殼一升一降，調理氣機：桔梗主升，枳殼主降。前胡與茯苓相配，善於祛痰兼健脾。生薑則能標本兼治，既能調中，又能袪散表邪。值得注意的是，此方藥味中有四分之三（人參、茯苓、甘草）出自四君子湯。加減葳蕤湯則治風熱外感兼陰虛內在之證。一般而言，外邪未解時不宜投以滋陰之藥，恐使邪氣內陷；然此方中滋養陰液津液，目的正是為引動汗出、透邪外達。透邪仍為此方之重點，同時兼顧滋陰。君藥玉竹滋養肺胃之陰而清熱，肺胃二臟為僅有的以津液為陰的臟腑，此藥實質上滋養肺胃之津液，且尤善於補而不滋膩，異於多數滋陰藥。諸臣藥解風熱之表（蔥白、桔梗、薄荷、淡豆豉）。白薇涼血而不傷陰津，其餘藥物則調和諸藥、潤燥。此二方皆治本虛之證（人參敗毒散為氣虛，加減葳蕤湯為陰津虛），且皆見無汗之表現。由於二者皆兼外感風邪，故發熱與惡寒並見，反映正邪相爭。《方劑學》，Bensky，第二版，第58頁"
    },
