      "exp_en": "This formula eliminates focal distention in the area below the heart (epigastrium). This is a variation of xiao chai hu tang appearing in the jin gui yao lue used for disorders appearing in the epigastrium. When there is distention in the middle jiao due to damp-heat in the middle burner ren shen, gan jiang, da zao, and zhi gan cao are omitted while zhi shi and sheng jiang are added. In this case, herbs that have a warming temperature can aggravate damp-heat. Also, tonifying methods are temporarily halted while the excess is eliminated. Tonification becomes secondary and qi regulation becomes the primary method of treatment. Ren shen is only slightly warm but it stokes the ming men fire causing fire in the body so it is eliminated. Gan jiang is hot and da zao also has a warming nature. Zhi gan cao can cause heat in the body and is tonifying with the potential to trap pathogens in the body so it is also eliminated. Zhi shi is a qi regulator which regulates the middle jiao and has a very strong downwards direction while sheng jiang has a slightly warm temperature (will not aggravate internal heat) while harmonizing the middle burner. Huang qin and huang lian cannot be eliminated from the formula or much of the power to dry, drain, and direct downwards is lost. Adding long dan cao to an already bitter and cold formula would make it too bitter and could cause damage to the middle jiao leading to further dampness. Mu dan pi cools the blood and invigorates the blood. This is not indicated for this situation. Bai wei clears heat from the blood and promotes urination while zhi zi clears toxic heat from the three burners and resolves dampness. Neither of these helps to regulate the qi of the middle burner. One of the things to remember is that in the presence of damp heat, the formula must change its nature and remove the warm or hot medicinals that can aggravate the condition from a temperature standpoint. Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 516, 30, 144, 126, 224, 95; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 127-130",
