      "exp_en": "This versatile formula is used to treat liver and gallbladder damp heat or liver fire. When there is severe headache and painful red eyes, liver fire is rising upwards causing these symptoms in the uppermost regions of the body. Ju hua and sang ye are added for their ability to clear and calm the liver, sedate heat toxins, and brighten the eyes. Ju hua is bitter, sweet, and slightly cold and pacifies and clears the liver while brightening the eyes. Sang ye is bitter, sweet, and cold to calm the liver and clear the eyes. Gou teng is sweet and slightly cold with the action of draining liver heat. It doesn't specifically work on the eyes as ju hua and sang ye do. Long dan cao and zhi zi are bitter and cold herbs in the formula to drain fire and eliminate dampness. These also do not specifically work on the eyes but rather drain fire systemically from the entire body. Yin chen hao is a bitter and slightly cold herb that clears heat, resolves dampness, and reduces jaundice. Its bitterness drains downwards but its slightly cold nature renders it less effective for the treatment of liver fire and it does not specifically treat the eyes. Chinese Herbal Medicine Materia Medica, Bensky, 2004, pgs 58, 54, 968, 310; Formulas and Strategies 2nd Edition, Bensky, 2009, pgs 199-201",
