      ],
      "exp_en": "COMMON: Both herbs activate blood circulation, remove blood stasis, and stop bleeding. DIFFERENCES: The herb San Qi enters the Liver and Stomach channels and strongly stops bleeding, relieves pains, invigorates blood, and promotes normal menstruation. It is sweet, slightly bitter, and warm. It enters the Liver and Stomach channels. The herb Pu Huang promotes urination and removes abdominal blood stasis. It is sweet and neutral. It enters the Liver and Pericardium channels. CAUTIONS/CONTRA: The herb San Qi should be used with caution during pregnancy. The herb Pu Huang is contraindicated during pregnancy as it can contract the uterus. It should not be used in the absence of blood stasis. DRUG INTERACTIONS: The herb San Qi, though undocumented, may interfere with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs because of the herb action to invigorate blood. The herb Pu Huang, though undocumented, may interfere with anticoagulants or antiplatelet drugs because of the herb has a hemostatic effect. Chinese Medical Herbology and Pharmacology, Chen and Chen, 2004, page 605, 587, 593",
      "exp_hant": "共同點：二藥皆能活血化瘀、止血。差異：三七入肝、胃二經，能強力止血、止痛、活血並調經；性甘微苦而溫，入肝、胃二經。蒲黃能利尿，並能消散腹中血瘀；性甘平，入肝、心包二經。禁忌：三七孕婦宜慎用。蒲黃孕婦禁用，因其能促使子宮收縮；無血瘀者不宜使用。藥物交互作用：三七雖未有明確記載，然因其活血之功，可能干擾抗凝血劑或抗血小板藥物。蒲黃雖未有明確記載，然因其具止血之效，亦可能干擾抗凝血劑或抗血小板藥物。Chinese Medical Herbology and Pharmacology, Chen and Chen, 2004, page 605, 587, 593"
