      "exp_en": "COMMON: All four herbs regulate qi and relieve pain. DIFFERENCES: The herb Xiang Fu regulates Liver qi, regulates menstruation, and is used mainly for obstetric/gynecological disorders. It is acrid, slightly bitter, slightly sweet, and neutral. It enters the Liver and Sanjiao channels. The herb Wu Yao enters the lower jiao, used for lower abdominal pain, and frequent urination. It is acrid and warm. It enters the Bladder, Kidney, and Lung channels. The herb Chen Xiang redirects qi to relieve nausea, cough, dyspnea, and vomiting. It is acrid, bitter, warm, and aromatic. It enters the Kidney, Spleen, and Stomach channels. The herb Chuan Lian Zi regulates Liver qi, used more for pain due to heat, and kills parasites. It is bitter and cold. It enters the Liver, Small Intestine, Stomach, and Bladder channels. CAUTIONS/CONTRA: The herb Xiang Fu should be used with caution with qi deficiency in the absence of stagnation or with yin deficiency with heat in the blood. The herb Wu Yao is contraindicated with qi and blood deficiencies or patients with internal heat because the herb has a warming drying effect. The herb Chen Xiang should be used with caution with yin deficiency fire or organ prolapse from qi deficiency. The herb Chuan Lian Zi should be used with caution with deficiency and cold of the Stomach and Spleen. Avoid large dosages. Chinese Medical Herbology and Pharmacology, Chen and Chen, 2004, page 514, 487, 495, 501",
      "exp_hant": "共同點：四藥皆能理氣止痛。差異：香附能疏肝理氣、調經，主要用於婦產科病證；性辛微苦微甘而平，入肝、三焦二經。烏藥入下焦，用於治療少腹疼痛及小便頻數；性辛溫，入膀胱、腎、肺三經。沉香能降逆氣以緩解噁心、咳嗽、氣喘與嘔吐；性辛苦溫而芳香，入腎、脾、胃三經。川楝子能疏肝理氣，多用於熱性疼痛，並能殺蟲；性苦寒，入肝、小腸、胃、膀胱四經。禁忌：香附於無氣滯之氣虛，或陰虛兼血熱者宜慎用。烏藥因其溫燥之性，禁用於氣血兩虛或內有熱象之患者。沉香於陰虛火旺，或氣虛所致臟器下垂者宜慎用。川楝子於脾胃虛寒者宜慎用，並應避免大劑量使用。Chinese Medical Herbology and Pharmacology, Chen and Chen, 2004, page 514, 487, 495, 501"
