        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "This patient has had multiple strokes and for the patient's current presentation, bu yang huan wu tang is the appropriate choice. This formula treats sequelae of brain trauma with patterns of qi and blood deficiency with blood stasis as well as wind-phlegm causing qi stagnation and blood stasis. Xue fu zhu yu tang treats blood stasis in the chest as well as qi stagnation. This is not the correct syndrome for the current presentation. Fu yuan huo xue tang treats blood stasis in the abdomen due to trauma which is not correct for this case. Tian ma gou teng yin is a more modern formula originally formulated to treat hypertension. From a pattern perspective, it treats liver yin deficiency with liver yang rising or liver yang rising generated internal wind. This is not appropriate for this case. Formulas and Strategies, 2nd Edition, Bensky, pgs. 564-567; 571-573; 647-649; 568-571",
      "exp_hant": "此患者已多次中風，就其目前的表現而言，補陽還五湯為適當之選。此方治療腦部外傷後遺症，證候包括氣血兩虛兼血瘀，以及風痰導致的氣滯血瘀。血府逐瘀湯治療胸中血瘀兼氣滯，並非本病例目前表現的正確證候。復元活血湯治療因外傷所致的腹部血瘀，並不適用於本病例。天麻鉤藤飲是較為現代的方劑，最初用於治療高血壓。從證候角度而言，此方治療肝陰虛兼肝陽上亢，或肝陽上亢化風所生之內風。此方並不適用於本病例。Formulas and Strategies, 2nd Edition, Bensky, pgs. 564-567; 571-573; 647-649; 568-571"
    },
    {
      "number": 763,
      "question_en": "A 13-year old boy comes to your office having suffered an asthma attack while playing soccer on a cool and windy fall afternoon. He reports taking two puffs from his inhaler and sitting for a half an hour before his mother drove him to your office. He reported that while running during the game he started having a coughing attack and coughed up yellow thick phlegm which he expelled. He was not able to run and had to be taken out of the game where he felt the need to utilize his inhaler (albuterol). He reports being chilled and he has a slightly elevated temperature (99.9 degrees). His pulse is slippery and rapid and his tongue has a greasy and slightly colored coating. You prescribe Ding Chuan Tang (Arrest Wheezing Decoction) for his condition. 7 days later, the young man comes to your office again and his mother reports that he felt much better on the formula but when he went back to school the same thing happened again in gym class when they were outside running track and field. For this visit, he reports the same symptoms and additionally he reports chest tightness which he reports as being \"very uncomfortable and makes breathing difficult\". How would you modify the original prescription for this new presentation?",
      "question_hant": "一位13歲男孩在一個涼爽多風的秋日下午踢足球時發作氣喘，前來你的診所就診。他表示曾使用兩劑吸入器，並靜坐半小時後，母親才開車帶他前來。他表示在比賽中跑步時開始劇烈咳嗽，並咳出黃色濃痰。他無法繼續跑動，只得退場，並感到需要使用吸入劑（沙丁胺醇）。他表示感到發冷，體溫略高（華氏99.9度，約攝氏37.7度）。脈滑數，舌苔膩且略帶顏色。你為其開立定喘湯治療。7天後，這位少年再度回診，其母表示他服藥後感覺明顯好轉，但回到學校在體育課於戶外進行田徑訓練跑步時，同樣的情況再度發作。此次回診，他表示症狀相同，並另外主訴胸悶，形容為「非常不舒服，且使呼吸變得困難」。針對此新的病情表現，你會如何調整原本的處方？",
      "image": null,
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "en": "Add Sheng Mai San (Generate the Pulse Powder)",
          "hant": "加生脈散",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "en": "Add Shi Gao (Gypsum Fibrosum) and Yu Xing Cao (Herba Houttuyniae)",
          "hant": "加石膏、魚腥草",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "en": "Add Zhi Shi (Fructus Aurantii Immaturus) and Hou Po (Cortex Magnoliae Officinalis)",
          "hant": "加枳實、厚朴",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "en": "Remove Bai Guo (Semen Gingko)",
          "hant": "去白果",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "exp_en": "This young man suffers from exercise induced asthma named xiao chuan in Chinese Medicine. There is a wind-cold attack with a concurrent phlegm-heat obstruction on the interior. The main issue from a Chinese medical standpoint is the disruption of the lung qi dynamic by the internal phlegm-heat. For this condition, ding chuan tang is the prescription. Upon his return, the young man reports the complication to the original presentation of severe chest distention and adding zhi shi and hou po is the correct modification. These two herbs open the chest, transform phlegm, and burst through the stagnation. Adding shi gao and yu xing cao is a modification of this formula in cases complicated with additional heat located in the lungs. The signs do not indicate that this is the case for this young man. Removing bai guo can be problematic as it works to temper against ma huang so that it does not become too strong in its ability to cause dispersing and depletion. This herb also transforms phlegm, preserves the lung qi and stops wheezing. Adding sheng mai san would only be indicated in cases where there is pronounced lung qi and yin deficiency. A predominant dry cough would be a sign to indicate the use of this formula. Formulas and Strategies, 2nd Edition, Bensky, pgs. 540-542",
